{
  "dom_id": "step-e12560",
  "name": "Example: Identifying Quadric Surfaces and Cylinders With Alternative Orientations",
  "problem": "Identify the surface $x = \\dfrac{y^2}{4} - 4z^2.$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nz = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}-\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}.\n$$\n\nThus, a hyperbolic paraboloid centered at $O$ whose axis of symmetry is the $\\boldsymbol x$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is a hyperbolic paraboloid whose axis of symmetry is the $x$-axis, and $b=2, \\: c=\\dfrac 12.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
